SYNC(1)		    UNIX Programmer's Manual		  SYNC(1)



NAME
     sync - force all filesystem state to disk

SYNOPSIS
     sync

DESCRIPTION
     _S_y_n_c executes the _s_y_n_c(2) primitive: every open file's
     buffered state is committed across every task, and then each
     mounted filesystem writes back the inodes its write path left
     dirty and the super block that block allocation left ahead of
     the disk.

     Use _s_y_n_c to insure that all disk writes have been completed
     before the machine is halted or the disk image is inspected
     from outside.

     A program that needs the same guarantee for ONE open file
     should call _f_s_y_n_c(_2) on its descriptor instead; a failed
     background flush latched on that file is reported there, and
     _s_y_n_c reports nothing.

     Nothing else on a running machine calls _s_y_n_c(2) on a clock.
     A super block reaches the disk when its allocation batch fills
     and an inode when its last reference goes, neither of which is
     a timer, so _u_p_d_a_t_e(8) runs this every thirty seconds out of
     /etc/rc.  _S_y_n_c is the same thing on demand.

SEE ALSO
     fsync(2), sync(2), update(8), halt(8)

